# Known Pitfalls

Documented traps encountered during implementation. Each entry names the symptom, root cause, and the fix, so a future agent (or human) lands the correct solution immediately.

---

## Riverpod

### `Bad state: Cannot use "ref" after the widget was disposed`

**Symptom:** App crashes when the user leaves a page while an async operation (e.g. `stop()`, `ref.read(...)`) is still in flight.

**Root cause:** `ConsumerStatefulWidget.dispose()` called `ref.read(provider.notifier).someAsyncMethod()`. The async continuations run after the widget tree detaches, at which point `ref` is invalid.

**Fix:** Use `autoDispose` on the provider and put all cleanup in `ref.onDispose` inside the controller's `build()`. Riverpod executes `onDispose` before invalidating `ref`, so it is always safe.

```dart
// controller
class MyController extends AutoDisposeNotifier<MyState> {
  @override
  MyState build() {
    ref.onDispose(() {
      // safe: ref is still valid here
      _subscription?.cancel();
      _resource.dispose();
    });
    return const MyState();
  }
}

final myProvider =
    NotifierProvider.autoDispose<MyController, MyState>(MyController.new);

// page — plain ConsumerWidget, no dispose() needed
class MyPage extends ConsumerWidget { ... }
```

**Never do:**
```dart
// ❌ ref.read() after widget disposed → crash
class _MyPageState extends ConsumerState<MyPage> {
  @override
  void dispose() {
    ref.read(myProvider.notifier).stop(); // async, ref will be dead
    super.dispose();
  }
}
```

